e knight with our pawn. That wins a knight for a pawn.  \n4. **N: g1-f3** – develops knight, controls center, prepares kingside castling.\n\nChecking the board again:  \nOn c6 there is NBF1 (black knight). Our d5 pawn is white (PWF1). d5-c6 is a valid move listed — that’s a pawn capture of the knight. That’s actually a tactical opportunity: we win a piece (knight) for free because Black’s knight is unprotected (I see no black pawn on b7 to recapture, and no other defender).  \n\nDouble-checking defenders: Black’s pawns are on a7, b7 missing?, c7 missing? Actually black pawns on a7, b7 (PBF0), c7 (PBF0) per row 7? Wait, board row 7: PBF0, PBF0, PBF0, empty, PBF0, empty, PBF0, PBF0. So b7 pawn is there. If we capture c6 knight with d5 pawn, b7 pawn can recapture c6. But b7 pawn is PBF0 (black pawn, not moved yet), so it *can* recapture. So it’s not a free piece — we trade pawn for knight after b7xc6. Still, that’s a knight for a pawn, which is good material gain.  \n\nComparing e4-f5 en passant: also good, but only wins a pawn. d5-c6 wins a knight (after recapture we lose our d5 pawn, but we get knight vs pawn).\n\nBetter to verify: d5-c6: pawn takes knight, then black can recapture with b7 pawn. Material: we lose d5 pawn (1 pawn), they lose knight (3 points), net +2 points advantage.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThe move d5-c6 is a clean tactical gain of material (knight for pawn), and it also opens the d-file for our rook later and removes a developed black knight that could support Black’s central play. In contrast, en passant e4-f5 only wins a pawn (1 point) but also gives us a strong pawn center after f5. Still, winning a knight is a bigger advantage. Developing moves like Nc3 are fine but miss this chance.  \n\nI’ll take the pawn capture on c6.  \n\n**Answer:** P: d5-c6'}
