F0, e8 empty, f8 BBF0, g8 NBF0, h8 RBF0. So e8 empty. So from f7, queen e6 check: e8 also available. So king could flee to e8 or g6. Still, queen check gains tempo and maybe sets up discovered check or mating net.\n\n**Promising moves:**  \n\n1. **Q: d5-e6+** — immediate check, forces black king to move, possibly opening up tactics because black king very exposed. After Ke8, our knight or bishop can develop with threats.  \n2. **B: c1-g5** — pins nothing yet but eyes f6, may prepare Qe6+ next turn with Bxf6+ possibility.  \n3. **P: e4-f5** — closes queen’s diagonal to f7, but gains space and threatens e6 later. Not as forcing.  \n\nBest is **Q: d5-e6+** because it forces black into awkward king move, possibly to e8, and then we can bring more pieces into attack (like Bg5 or Nf3).  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThe black king is exposed on f7, a huge tactical vulnerability. Direct check with queen forces it to move and disrupts black’s coordination. It also potentially wins the pawn on d6 if black blunders (but here black can defend). Most importantly, it maintains initiative and keeps black on defensive while our undeveloped pieces can join the attack quickly.  \n\n**Answer:** Q: d5-e6'}
